all right um this is something a little  different you know i get  so many emails probably like eight to  ten emails a week from people who either  want to start a podcast youtube channel  or independent media show  or who have started it but it's not  going as well as they want and they want  advice from me  and it's beyond the scope of the 24  hours that i have in the day  to to be doing too much of this kind of  individual guidance but there  are a bunch of different people over the  years that have come to me for  advice people i know personally uh or  people that are introduced  to me and um they often have very  similar questions and the point of  failure for almost everybody is exactly  the same  and i sort of tweeted about this  yesterday and a bunch of people wrote  back and said you should really kind of  talk about this  um being you know as not we're i believe  in the top  point one percent of youtube at this  point and uh having done it and having  gotten to the point of hiring staff and  having a successful business model  what is it that causes people to fail  and unfortunately  fortunately or unfortunately the the  good i have good news and bad news and  it's all the same  the biggest single point of failure is  essentially not showing up  um when i will talk to people who have  started shows or want to  they often will jump to things like how  do i negotiate a brand  deal what should my youtube thumbnails  look like  what kind of equipment do i need for my  podcast when can i  monetize and so often the cart is being  put before the horse  and what i tell everybody is all of that  stuff  will come naturally you'll figure that  stuff out when the time comes  once you are actually steadily and  consistently and reliably producing the  content this  this isn't like the bogus 98 is showing  up  this is just the reality that people  will often focus on things that aren't  the most important now obviously  if your content is terrible some people  just like  they're you know the content's not good  assuming  that your content isn't terrible where  almost  everybody fails is they get discouraged  too early there are overnight successes  in independent media of course there are  um but the vast majority are not  and people will come to me and i'll say  you need to pick a content schedule and  stick to it are you doing a weekly show  are you doing daily youtube clips are  you doing a twice a week show  or what is the format and the timeline  that you are  committing to and sometimes people will  hear that  um and and they will keep it going you  know i remember in fact  years and years ago meeting up with kyle  kalinski when both of our channels were  much smaller  and the thing we talked about as we both  were looking to grow  at the time that he actually did and he  now has a channel that is doing really  well  is you've got to commit to the schedule  of content that's actually the key thing  we didn't talk about  monetization brand deals thumbnails we  we  talked maybe about a you know what  microphone do you use but it was all  about the content and so i'll have  conversations with people  and ninety percent of the conversation  is about things that don't really make a  difference  and then they'll say yeah yeah yeah i'm  gonna do the content six months go by  i'll come across their youtube channel  and it'll say oh their their latest  video is six weeks old  you've done no content for six weeks and  sometimes we'll have another interaction  and they'll say  yeah you know it just wasn't as  lucrative as i wanted it to be it's like  well but you're not publishing the  content  you have to do that first the other big  mistake  is sometimes just scaling up expenses  before it's time  i know of shows that are one-tenth the  size of our show  where the host will tell me they already  have  an executive assistant who responds to  their email and schedules their  interviews  what what that doesn't make any sense  and i from a business standpoint i've  talked before  about the push cart method one of my mba  professors would talk about the push  cart method  don't necessarily go to a restaurant  with an expensive lease and tons of  employees  try the food with a push cart a food  cart and if it does well then you roll  that into maybe getting the restaurant  and this is like  the the most important thing that i that  i tell people and most of them fail to  do it and there are tons of shows that  could do fine the content's good  um but people just don't show up and so  you know unfortunately i i at this point  i'm unable to provide individualized  advice  to all the people who email me but if  you're not doing the content  don't be surprised that none of the  other stuff is going as well as you want  it to go  hopefully that's useful to some people  and i think that that's what we  committed to early with the show which  was when we were twice a week  we were twice a week and we were doing  the content and we were putting up the  clips in the podcast episodes  when we went to when when that was  supported  financially we then went to four times a  week  and we did it and then when that was  supported financially we went to the  five  and people can count on the content it's  the most important thing  it's the biggest point of failure and  the real reason why tons of people i  mean  it's dozens and dozens of people over  time who have emailed me saying  i want to do what you're doing but it's  just not happening you didn't do the  main thing i said which is commit to the  content schedule
 

